# AI Cultivation RPG - Development Rules

## General Philosophy

This project is a long-term simulation RPG.

Prioritize correctness, scalability, and maintainability. Never prioritize speed over architecture.

## Before Making Changes

- Read every document in `docs/` before making changes.
- Treat `docs/` as the authoritative source of truth.
- If documentation and code disagree, ask before changing either.

## Coding Standards

- Use Python 3.14+ (see docs/TECH_STACK.md)
- Use type hints everywhere
- Write docstrings for public classes and functions
- Prefer readability over clever code
- Avoid global variables
- Use dependency injection where practical
- Never hardcode magic numbers
- Never duplicate logic

## Architecture

Keep responsibilities separate. Never mix them.

| Layer | Owns |
|-------|------|
| **Game Engine** | Rules, calculations, time, combat, cultivation, economy |
| **Database** | Persistent state only |
| **AI** | Narration, dialogue, flavor text |

## AI Rules

AI never decides combat, money, cultivation, inventory, relationships, or database edits.

AI only proposes narration. The engine decides all mechanics.

## Database

Everything important must have a permanent unique ID: players, NPCs, items, techniques, quests, sects, buildings, locations—everything.

Never delete IDs.

## Implementation

- Implement only the requested feature
- Never rewrite working systems unless asked
- Never redesign architecture without approval
- Never delete files without approval

## Testing

- Every new system requires tests
- Run tests before marking work complete
- If tests fail, fix them first

## Documentation

When architecture or a system changes, update `docs/` in the same change. Keep docs synchronized with code.

## Performance

Design for eventual scale: millions of events, thousands of NPCs, tens of thousands of techniques, hundreds of sects.

## Communication

- Explain major design decisions
- List every file modified
- State assumptions
- Never claim complete work that is only partially implemented
